course: dictionary
lesson: 12423
title: Words that dazzle
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Dazzled» can mean two different things, and the sentence tells you which one. «Mia was dazzled by the bright sun as she came out of the cave, and she could not see the path.» The light stopped her seeing clearly for a moment.
explain: «Dazzled» can also mean very impressed. «The crowd was dazzled by the fireworks over the river.» Nobody was blinded here, so the crowd must have been amazed. Check by swapping in a simpler word: «The crowd was amazed by the fireworks» still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
explain: Dazzle is also a verb. «The sun dazzled the walkers» means the sun made them unable to see clearly, or amazed them. Dazzled is its past form, so it tells you the thing happened earlier.
fact: «Dazzled» means unable to see clearly for a moment because of a very bright light.
fact: «Dazzled» can also mean very impressed by something amazing, beautiful or clever.
fact: Dazzled is the past form of the verb dazzle: «The sun dazzled the drivers.»
fact: Bright headlights or low sun can dazzle drivers and cyclists, so never shine lasers or torches into people's eyes.
fact: Old warships once used dazzle camouflage, bold stripes that were painted to confuse enemies.
exercise: «I was dazzled by the car's headlights on the dark road.» What does dazzled mean here? | Covered in mud | *Unable to see clearly for a moment because of bright light | Fast and quiet | Very impressed by the car
exercise: «The audience was dazzled by the acrobats.» What does dazzled mean? | Sleepy after a long show | *Very impressed by something amazing | Bored and yawning | Scared of the dark
exercise: Which word in «The sun dazzled the drivers» is the verb in the past form? | drivers | *dazzled | the | sun
test: Which phrase is called dazzle camouflage, and what was it used for? | *Bold stripes painted on warships to confuse enemies | Soft music to calm the sailors | Loud horns to warn ships | Hidden tunnels under the sea
test: Why must you never shine a torch into someone's eyes? | It makes the torch work better | It makes them very impressed | *It can dazzle them and stop them seeing clearly | It warms their faces
exam: In «The fireworks dazzled the crowd», what does dazzled most likely mean? | Stopped the fireworks from going off | Made the crowd bored | *Amazed the crowd, leaving them very impressed | Made the crowd fall asleep
